diff --git a/docs/decisions/0001-overdue-grace-period.md b/docs/decisions/0001-overdue-grace-period.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8e42b9c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/0001-overdue-grace-period.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# 0001 - A loan becomes overdue after a two-day grace period
+
+Status: accepted, 2026-09-01
+
+## Context
+
+Members often bring items back a day or two after the due date, usually because the desk was
+closed. Today a loan counts as overdue the day after its due date, so the overdue report fills up
+with loans nobody needs to chase and the desk has stopped reading it.
+
+## Decision
+
+- A loan that has not been returned is **overdue only once it is more than two days past its due
+  date**. A loan due on the 10th is still active on the 11th and the 12th, and overdue from the 13th.
+  The number of days lives in `LendingPolicy.OverdueGraceDays`.
+- This is what 'overdue' means everywhere in the API: a loan's `status`, the `status=Overdue` and
+  `status=Active` filters on `GET /api/loans`, the overdue report, and anything added after this
+  change that looks at overdue loans.
+- How many days overdue a loan is still counts from its due date: on the 13th, the loan above is
+  3 days overdue.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The worker's overdue notice still goes out the day after the due date for now. It moves to the
+  grace period in a separate change, once the notice text mentions the grace period.
